<title>CZECH REPUBLIC: Czech airline CSA posts 1996 audited net loss.</title>
<headline>Czech airline CSA posts 1996 audited net loss.</headline>
<dateline>PRAGUE 1997-07-23</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Czech national airline Ceske Aerolinie a.s. (CSA) posted a 1996 after-tax loss according to international accounting standards (IAS) of 249 million crowns after a 224 million crown profit the previous year, the company said in a statement on Wednesday.  </p>
<p>    turnover (czk million)	 9,088	vs    8,512</p>
<p>    operating result	     339	vs	313</p>
<p>    pre-tax profit		-242	vs	208</p>
<p>    after-tax profit	    -249	vs	224</p>
<p>    equity			 1,211	vs    1,460</p>
<p>    registered capital	 2,736	vs    2,736</p>
<p>Note. Results audited according to international accounting standards. CSA is owned by the Czech government and the state-run consolidation bank, with a minority share held by the European Bank for Reconstruction and Development (EBRD).</p>
<p>The company said 1996 results were adversely affected by a rise in the price of aviation fuel which made for most of the volume of the loss. TRANSPORT RESULTS</p>
<p>    tonne-km utilised    (million)    268.7    vs     264.4</p>
<p>    tonne-km available   (million)    479.1    vs     461.3</p>
<p>    number of passengers	1,612,100    vs  1,488,300</p>
<p>    cargo carried   (tonnes)     10,000    vs     10,600</p>
<p>total load factor (pct)</p>
<p>	 weight			    56.2    vs	57.3</p>
<p>	 seat				66.0    vs	66.6</p>
<p>    average number of employees   3,976    vs     3,916</p>
<p>-- John Mastrini, Prague Newsroom, 420-2-2423-0003</p>
